{"causes": ["The ongoing Eighty Years' War between the Spanish Empire and the Dutch Republic created a constant demand for skilled military commanders.", "Frederik inherited his title and military tradition from the noble House of Van den Bergh, which had deep roots in the Low Countries.", "His loyalty to the Spanish Habsburg Crown positioned him as a defender of Catholic interests against the Protestant-led Dutch rebellion."], "effects": ["Frederik van den Bergh's military actions prolonged Spanish control over strategic areas in the eastern Low Countries.", "His involvement in numerous sieges and skirmishes contributed to the high human and material cost of the Eighty Years' War.", "His career exemplified the challenges faced by noble families caught between competing political and religious loyalties during the conflict."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "eighty-years-war", "sourceName": "Eighty Years' War", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "frederik-van-den-bergh", "targetName": "Frederik van den Bergh", "context": "Frederik van den Bergh's entire military career and historical significance are encapsulated within the timeframe and events of the Eighty Years' War (1568-1648)."}, {"sourceSlug": "frederik-van-den-bergh", "sourceName": "Frederik van den Bergh", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "eighty-years-war", "targetName": "Eighty Years' War", "context": "Frederik van den Bergh was a dedicated soldier and commander for the Spanish forces throughout the Eighty Years' War."}, {"sourceSlug": "frederik-van-den-bergh", "sourceName": "Frederik van den Bergh", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "boxmeer-netherlands", "targetName": "Boxmeer, Netherlands", "context": "Frederik van den Bergh held the title of Lord of Boxmeer, a strategic lordship in the eastern Low Countries."}, {"sourceSlug": "spanish-empire", "sourceName": "Spanish Empire", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "frederik-van-den-bergh", "targetName": "Frederik van den Bergh", "context": "Frederik van den Bergh's military service and noble loyalties were primarily directed towards the interests of the Spanish Habsburg Monarchy."}, {"sourceSlug": "maurice-of-nassau", "sourceName": "Maurice of Nassau", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "frederik-van-den-bergh", "targetName": "Frederik van den Bergh", "context": "The military campaigns and strategic maneuvers of Maurice of Nassau often directly opposed and challenged Frederik van den Bergh's command during the Eighty Years' War."}], "places": [{"name": "Boxmeer, Netherlands", "role": "Lordship & military base"}, {"name": "Low Countries", "role": "Primary theater of action"}, {"name": "Spanish Netherlands", "role": "Area of loyal service"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Frederik van den Bergh", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "frederik-van-den-bergh"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q699180", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederik_van_den_Bergh"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Frederik van den Bergh was a significant regional military commander whose actions helped maintain Spanish influence in parts of the Low Countries, contributing to the prolonged and costly nature of the Eighty Years' War.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-18T00:44:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35292205464", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Frederik van den Bergh was a soldier in the Eighty Years' War. His titles included Lord of Boxmeer.", "newValue": "Frederik van den Bergh (died 1618-09-03) was a prominent military commander and nobleman in the Spanish Low Countries, whose career was defined by his dedicated service to the Spanish Crown during the tumultuous Eighty Years' War (1568–1648). As Lord of Boxmeer and a member of the influential House of Van den Bergh, he inherited a legacy of military prowess and political entanglement in the region. His actions contributed significantly to the protracted conflict between the nascent Dutch Republic and the Spanish Habsburgs, making him a key figure in the struggle for control over strategic territories in the Low Countries.

Van den Bergh distinguished himself through several military campaigns and sieges. Notably, he commanded forces defending Spanish interests in the eastern parts of the Netherlands, often clashing with the armies of Maurice of Nassau, Prince of Orange. His strategic importance lay in holding key strongholds and disrupting Dutch supply lines, participating in engagements that shaped the territorial boundaries and military strategies of the early 17th century. He was instrumental in maintaining Spanish influence in areas like the Peel and Maas region, a vital corridor for both Spanish and Dutch movements.

His death in 1618, just before the Twelve Years' Truce concluded, marked the end of a career that exemplified the complex loyalties and constant warfare of the Early Modern period in Europe. While his individual campaigns might not have decisively altered the war's outcome, his persistent military efforts contributed to the immense cost and duration of the Eighty Years' War, indirectly influencing the eventual recognition of the Dutch Republic's independence. His legacy is embedded in the military history of the Low Countries, reflecting the enduring struggle between dynastic power and emerging national identities."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-18T00:44:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35292205464", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1559-08-18, Ulft"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1559-08-18, Ulft"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Eighty Years' War between the Spanish Empire and the Dutch Republic created a constant demand for skilled military commanders.", "Frederik inherited his title and military tradition from the noble House of Van den Bergh, which had deep roots in the Low Countries.", "His loyalty to the Spanish Habsburg Crown positioned him as a defender of Catholic interests against the Protestant-led Dutch rebellion."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-18T00:44:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35292205464", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1618-09-03"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1618-09-03"}], "newValue": ["Frederik van den Bergh's military actions prolonged Spanish control over strategic areas in the eastern Low Countries.", "His i